(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-beta-calcium-blockers-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Class II & IV: Beta Blockers & Calcium Channel Blockers Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which condition can a beta-1 selective blocker such as metoprolol still worsen?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia","Glaucoma","A seizure disorder","Congestive heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol and acebutolol cause less bronchospasm, but congestive heart failure remains on their list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why is propranolol a concern in a patient with asthma?","choices":["It can cause pneumonitis","It can cause respiratory paralysis","It can cause pulmonary fibrosis","It can cause bronchospasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol is nonselective, and bronchospasm is on its toxicity list; a beta-1 selective blocker causes less. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which channel do class IV antiarrhythmics block?","choices":["The L-type calcium channel","The fast sodium channel","The potassium efflux channel","The sodium-potassium pump"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class IV drugs block the L-type calcium channel, decrease automaticity, slow atrioventricular node conduction and are negative inotropes. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Where are verapamil and diltiazem most effective?","choices":["In ischemic tissue","In normal ventricular muscle","In the most active tissue","In tissue at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem block both activated and inactivated channels and are most effective in the most active tissue. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Class II antiarrhythmics slow the heart mainly by which effect on pacemaker cells?","choices":["Decreasing phase 4 depolarization","Slowing the phase 0 sodium upstroke","Prolonging phase 3 repolarization","Shortening phase 3 repolarization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class II drugs decrease phase 4 depolarization, so pacemaker cells reach threshold later; they also slow conduction and prolong atrioventricular conduction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Metoprolol and acebutolol are which type of beta blocker?","choices":["Nonselective","Beta-1 selective","Beta blockers that block potassium channels","Beta blockers that block sodium channels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol and acebutolol are beta-1 selective blockers, similar to propranolol but with less bronchospasm. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which of these beta blockers is nonselective?","choices":["Metoprolol","Acebutolol","Propranolol","Esmolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol is a nonselective beta blocker, which is why it carries the bronchospasm risk. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which arrhythmia is a listed indication for verapamil and diltiazem?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Torsades de pointes","Ventricular fibrillation","Supraventricular tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem are listed for supraventricular tachycardia (paroxysmal) and atrial fibrillation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which adverse effects are expected with verapamil and diltiazem?","choices":["Bronchospasm and fatigue","Hypotension and edema","Flushing and chest burning","Nystagmus and seizures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension and edema are the listed toxicities of verapamil and diltiazem. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects are listed for metoprolol and acebutolol?","choices":["Seizures and nystagmus","Sedation and fatigue","Tinnitus and blurred vision","Neuropathy and ataxia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Central nervous system sedation and fatigue are listed for metoprolol and acebutolol, with congestive heart failure. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which conduction disorder is a listed toxicity of propranolol?","choices":["Heart block","Sinus tachycardia","QT prolongation","Torsades de pointes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol slows atrioventricular conduction, and heart block is on its toxicity list with heart failure and hypotension. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do verapamil and diltiazem do to the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes?","choices":["Speed both the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes","Hyperpolarize both nodes through potassium channels","Suppress the sinoatrial node, slow the atrioventricular node","Shorten phase 3 in both nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem suppress the sinoatrial node and decrease atrioventricular conduction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 38"}]};
